„­bible_version£KJV¤book+§chapter¨sections—…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd Paul, earnestly beholding the council, said, Men and brethren, I have lived in all good conscience before God until this day.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWAnd the high priest Ananias commanded them that stood by him to smite him on the mouth.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¡Then said Paul unto him, God shall smite thee, thou whited wall: for sittest thou to judge me after the law, and commandest me to be smitten contrary to the law?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ=And they that stood by said, Revilest thou God's high priest?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‹Then said Paul, I wist not, brethren, that he was the high priest: for it is written, Thou shalt not speak evil of the ruler of thy people.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse
¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙëBut when Paul perceived that the one part were Sadducees, and the other Pharisees, he cried out in the council, Men and brethren, I am a Pharisee, the son of a Pharisee: of the hope and resurrection of the dead I am called in question.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙyAnd when he had so said, there arose a dissension between the Pharisees and the Sadducees: and the multitude was divided.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙoFor the Sadducees say that there is no resurrection, neither angel, nor spirit: but the Pharisees confess both.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse	¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÓAnd there arose a great cry: and the scribes that were of the Pharisees' part arose, and strove, saying, We find no evil in this man: but if a spirit or an angel hath spoken to him, let us not fight against God.¦layout ªlast_verse	„¥verse
¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙçAnd when there arose a great dissension, the chief captain, fearing lest Paul should have been pulled in pieces of them, commanded the soldiers to go down, and to take him by force from among them, and to bring him into the castle.¦layout ªlast_verse
…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses‘„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ§And the night following the Lord stood by him, and said, Be of good cheer, Paul: for as thou hast testified of me in Jerusalem, so must thou bear witness also at Rome.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¥And when it was day, certain of the Jews banded together, and bound themselves under a curse, saying that they would neither eat nor drink till they had killed Paul.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ=And they were more than forty which had made this conspiracy.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ˜And they came to the chief priests and elders, and said, We have bound ourselves under a great curse, that we will eat nothing until we have slain Paul.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙâNow therefore ye with the council signify to the chief captain that he bring him down unto you to morrow, as though ye would enquire something more perfectly concerning him: and we, or ever he come near, are ready to kill him.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses—„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙnAnd when Paul's sister's son heard of their lying in wait, he went and entered into the castle, and told Paul.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙThen Paul called one of the centurions unto him, and said, Bring this young man unto the chief captain: for he hath a certain thing to tell him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ»So he took him, and brought him to the chief captain, and said, Paul the prisoner called me unto him, and prayed me to bring this young man unto thee, who hath something to say unto thee.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙThen the chief captain took him by the hand, and went with him aside privately, and asked him, What is that thou hast to tell me?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¬And he said, The Jews have agreed to desire thee that thou wouldest bring down Paul to morrow into the council, as though they would enquire somewhat of him more perfectly.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙşBut do not thou yield unto them: for there lie in wait for him of them more than forty men, which have bound themselves with an oath, that they will neither eat nor drink till they have killed him: and now are they ready, looking for a promise from thee.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙƒSo the chief captain then let the young man depart, and charged him, See thou tell no man that thou hast shewed these things to me.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses˜„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¼And he called unto him two centurions, saying, Make ready two hundred soldiers to go to CÃ¦sarea, and horsemen threescore and ten, and spearmen two hundred, at the third hour of the night;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ_and provide them beasts, that they may set Paul on, and bring him safe unto Felix the governor.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ(And he wrote a letter after this manner:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙHClaudius Lysias unto the most excellent governor Felix sendeth greeting.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ–This man was taken of the Jews, and should have been killed of them: then came I with an army, and rescued him, having understood that he was a Roman.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙiAnd when I would have known the cause wherefore they accused him, I brought him forth into their council:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ}whom I perceived to be accused of questions of their law, but to have nothing laid to his charge worthy of death or of bonds.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¾And when it was told me how that the Jews laid wait for the man, I sent straightway to thee, and gave commandment to his accusers also to say before thee what they had against him. Farewell.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse#¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ_Then the soldiers, as it was commanded them, took Paul, and brought him by night to Antipatris.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙPOn the morrow they left the horsemen to go with him, and returned to the castle:¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙkwho, when they came to CÃ¦sarea, and delivered the epistle to the governor, presented Paul also before him.¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ{And when the governor had read the letter, he asked of what province he was. And when he understood that he was of Cilicia;¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙwI will hear thee, said he, when thine accusers are also come. And he commanded him to be kept in Herod's judgment hall.¦layout ªlast_verse#